  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1118 AM MST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nACTIVE WEATHER TODAY: \n\nA fast moving low pressure system currently centered over\neastern Utah will bring rain and mountain snow to our region\nthrough very early tomorrow morning. Rivers in southwest \nColorado and southeast Utah that have continued flooding \nconcerns from the recent prolonged rainfall event, such as the \nSan Juan River, the Mancos River, and the Los Pinos River, are \nnot expected to be impacted much if at all from today's \nrainfall as even high end (1 in 10 chance) forecasts have the \nliquid precipitation at or below 0.3 inches in the Four Corners \narea. However, there is potential for stronger convection, \nparticularly along the Utah/Colorado border, that does increase \nconcerns for localized flash flooding. Especially if storms \nproducing heavier rainfall develop over flood-prone or already \nsaturated areas. \n\nSnowfall is expected through very early tomorrow morning, with\nthe strongest snowfall rates forecast to occur this evening over\nthe central Colorado mountains. A Winter Weather Advisory\nremains in effect this afternoon through 5 AM tomorrow morning \nfor a portion of Colorado's central mountains, including the \nGore and Elk Mountains, above 10,000 feet for 4 to 8 inches of\nsnow. Snow levels are forecast to drop to around 7,000 feet \ntonight, but accumulations below 10,000 feet are expected to\nonly be around 1 to 4 inches. Use caution if traveling as\nhazardous conditions could develop.\n\nDRIER WEATHER THROUGH THE WEEKEND: \n\nBeyond Thursday morning, the forecast overall looks to trend\nmuch drier. A quick moving shortwave skirts the region across\nthe north on Friday which could bring some increased clouds and\na few light rain or snow showers over the Divide. A broad ridge\nof high pressure moves in late this week into the coming \nweekend, with temperatures warming to above seasonable levels \nand providing a much needed break from the recent wet weather.",
